  "subtitle": "The console that made a telephone keypad a game controller and let a printed paper card redefine every button",
  "why_scout_cares": "The Intellivision's distinctive interaction is its hand controller: a 12-button telephone-style keypad whose function is reprogrammed by sliding a printed paper overlay over it — plus a free-spinning 16-direction disc for movement. Every cartridge shipped with two plastic overlays that turn one generic numeric keypad into a game-specific command surface (the museum's only 'printed paper redefines a hardware keyboard' interface, cousin to the HP-150 touch template and Sharp Wizard cards but as a game input). The wandering disc, the telephone keypad, and the overlay-templated buttons together make it the museum's first overlay-programmable console controller, distinct from every other console input in the collection.",
